1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:02,510
Your weather app does not own a single weather

2
00:00:02,510 --> 00:00:05,020
station. So how does it know it is raining

3
00:00:05,020 --> 00:00:07,530
in Nairobi right now? The answer is something you

4
00:00:07,530 --> 00:00:10,039
use hundreds of times a day without noticing: an

5
00:00:10,039 --> 00:00:12,549
API. By the end of this video, you will

6
00:00:12,549 --> 00:00:13,944
understand exactly what that is.

7
00:00:14,344 --> 00:00:16,685
Imagine you are at a restaurant. You are hungry,

8
00:00:16,685 --> 00:00:19,026
but you cannot walk into the kitchen and start

9
00:00:19,026 --> 00:00:21,368
cooking. You also do not need to know how

10
00:00:21,368 --> 00:00:23,709
the kitchen works. You just need a way to

11
00:00:23,709 --> 00:00:26,051
ask for food and get it back. That is

12
00:00:26,051 --> 00:00:28,392
the waiter. You give the waiter your order, the

13
00:00:28,392 --> 00:00:30,734
waiter carries it to the kitchen, the kitchen prepares

14
00:00:30,734 --> 00:00:32,815
it, and the waiter brings your meal back.

15
00:00:33,215 --> 00:00:36,412
An API, short for Application Programming Interface, is the

16
00:00:36,412 --> 00:00:39,609
waiter. It is the messenger between two pieces of

17
00:00:39,609 --> 00:00:42,806
software. One side asks. The other side answers. And

18
00:00:42,806 --> 00:00:46,002
the one asking never has to see what happens

19
00:00:46,002 --> 00:00:46,358
inside.

20
00:00:46,758 --> 00:00:49,444
Now let's swap the restaurant for the real thing.

21
00:00:49,444 --> 00:00:52,131
The customer is your weather app. The kitchen is

22
00:00:52,131 --> 00:00:54,818
a weather company's server, which holds all the data.

23
00:00:54,818 --> 00:00:57,505
The waiter is the API. Step one is the

24
00:00:57,505 --> 00:01:00,192
request. Your app sends a message that says, give

25
00:01:00,192 --> 00:01:02,879
me the weather for Nairobi, in a format the

26
00:01:02,879 --> 00:01:05,566
server understands. Step two: the API receives it, checks

27
00:01:05,566 --> 00:01:08,253
that the request is valid, and often checks that

28
00:01:08,253 --> 00:01:09,745
you are allowed to ask.

29
00:01:10,145 --> 00:01:13,386
Step three: the server does the work. It looks

30
00:01:13,386 --> 00:01:16,627
up the data and prepares an answer. Step four

31
00:01:16,627 --> 00:01:19,868
is the response. The API carries the answer back,

32
00:01:19,868 --> 00:01:23,109
usually in a format called JSON, which is simply

33
00:01:23,109 --> 00:01:26,350
neatly organized text: city, Nairobi. Temperature, twenty four degrees.

34
00:01:26,350 --> 00:01:29,591
Condition, light rain. Then your app turns that data

35
00:01:29,591 --> 00:01:32,111
into the icons and numbers you see.

36
00:01:32,511 --> 00:01:35,121
Notice what did not happen. Your app never touched

37
00:01:35,121 --> 00:01:37,731
the weather company's database. It never needed to know

38
00:01:37,731 --> 00:01:40,340
how the data was collected. It only knew how

39
00:01:40,340 --> 00:01:42,950
to ask, and how to read the answer. That

40
00:01:42,950 --> 00:01:44,400
separation is the whole point.

41
00:01:44,800 --> 00:01:47,572
This is why APIs are everywhere. When you pay

42
00:01:47,572 --> 00:01:50,345
online, a shop talks to your bank through an

43
00:01:50,345 --> 00:01:53,117
API. When you log in with Google, a website

44
00:01:53,117 --> 00:01:55,889
talks to Google through an API. When a travel

45
00:01:55,889 --> 00:01:58,662
site compares flights, it asks dozens of airline APIs

46
00:01:58,662 --> 00:01:59,278
at once.

47
00:01:59,678 --> 00:02:02,249
Let's see how little code it takes. This free,

48
00:02:02,249 --> 00:02:04,820
public API returns a random joke. A few lines

49
00:02:04,820 --> 00:02:07,391
of Python ask a server across the world a

50
00:02:07,391 --> 00:02:09,962
question and get an answer back. What comes back

51
00:02:09,962 --> 00:02:12,533
is not a pretty page. It is raw data,

52
00:02:12,533 --> 00:02:15,104
a setup and a punchline, and apps decorate it.

53
00:02:15,104 --> 00:02:17,104
That is the power of an API.

54
00:02:17,504 --> 00:02:20,594
Let's recap in three points. One: an API is

55
00:02:20,594 --> 00:02:23,683
a messenger that lets two programs talk without seeing

56
00:02:23,683 --> 00:02:26,773
each other's insides. Two: it works on request and

57
00:02:26,773 --> 00:02:29,863
response, like ordering and receiving food. Three: almost every

58
00:02:29,863 --> 00:02:32,609
app you use depends on dozens of them.

59
00:02:33,009 --> 00:02:35,979
Next time, we answer the question this video quietly

60
00:02:35,979 --> 00:02:38,950
raised: when your app sends that request, how does

61
00:02:38,950 --> 00:02:41,920
it physically travel across the world? That is how

62
00:02:41,920 --> 00:02:44,891
the internet works. If this helped, subscribe, and comment

63
00:02:44,891 --> 00:02:47,861
with the concept you want explained next. See you

64
00:02:47,861 --> 00:02:49,181
in the next one.

